  "decision": {
    "answer": "REVISIONS",
    "explanation": "The claim posits that TERT promoter mutations have a negative diagnostic significance in thyroid angiosarcoma. The available evidence from pmid-23766237 does not mention thyroid angiosarcoma; it reports on benign thyroid tumors, papillary, follicular, poorly differentiated, and anaplastic thyroid cancers. Therefore, the disease in the claim is not supported by the evidence. The evidence supports the presence of TERT promoter mutations in aggressive thyroid cancers (like ATC), but the specific disease entity 'thyroid angiosarcoma' is absent. Thus, the claim requires revision to reflect the diseases actually studied.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-23766237",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 580,
        "quote": "We found the C228T mutation in 0 of 85 (0.0%) benign thyroid tumors, 30 of 257 (11.7%) papillary thyroid cancers (PTC), 9 of 79 (11.4%) follicular thyroid cancers (FTC), 3 of 8 (37.5%) poorly differentiated thyroid cancers (PDTC), 23 of 54 (42.6%) anaplastic thyroid cancers (ATC), and 8 of 12 (66.7%) thyroid cancer cell lines. . . . As shown in Table 3, TERT promoter mutations were significantly more prevalent in the TCPTC samples than in the CPTC and FVPTC samples, 30.8% (4/13) in the former vs 10.3% (25/243) in the latter two (P=0.046, per two-tailed Fisher's exact test)."
      }
    ],
